# Sunrise Additional Terms 1.0

These terms apply together with the PolyForm Shield License 1.0.0 below ("Shield"). Each contributor is the
licensor of their own contribution and offers it under Shield with these terms. These terms add permissions
to Shield and take nothing away from it.

## 1. Scope

"The software" means everything in this repository except:

a. what was already in it at commit de8b4e2526df9d7f611515b78123a26a3914176f, the last code taken from
   Horizon and ari's fork. That stays under the licences it had there: the MIT licence in
   LICENSES/MIT-Horizon.txt, or for third-party parts the licence THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md names. Only later
   changes to it are the software.
b. code taken later from other projects, which LICENSES/IMPORTS lists with its source and licence.

"The Project" means Sunrise as developed at https://github.com/sunrise-org/sunrise, or at the place its
GOVERNANCE.md names as its successor.

"Your group" means you, your affiliates, and anyone who can publish or sign releases of a fork, or of a
product the fork is shipped in or started from, or who shares with you in money made from either.

## 2. Always allowed

None of the following counts as competing under Shield's Noncompete, whoever does it, a fork's own makers
included, and whether or not they charge for it:

a. Playing, and running, hosting or renting out game servers, changed for your own operation or not,
   including selling things on them.
b. Making and sharing mods, plugins, packs, shaders and tools that run inside the software or a fork allowed
   by section 3, or work with it through its mod API, network protocol or file formats. They may contain
   whatever the Project's SDK, templates and build tools put into them, but not the game itself, and need not
   carry these terms or the Required Notice for it.
c. Videos, streams, guides, reviews and links about the software or about a fork.
d. Carrying copies for others without changing them, charging for them or passing them off as your own:
   code hosts, package registries, app stores, mirrors. The ads and sign-ins such a carrier applies to
   everything it carries do not count against a fork or server build it carries, unless those who make it
   take a share of the money from them.
e. The Project itself, for as long as it meets 3a to 3d. For the Project, Shield's 32 days to fix apply to
   the first and the second violation it is notified of, not only the first. Notices about the same violation
   count once, and a notice reaches the Project as an issue opened in its repository.
f. Handing dedicated server software to others, for as long as it costs nothing to get or use, getting it is
   not gated by ads or sign-ups, and it meets 3a and 3e as a fork would. A dedicated server serves the game
   to clients over the network and cannot itself be used to play. Charging to run or host one is covered
   by 2a.

## 3. Free forks

You may also provide a product that Shield's Noncompete would otherwise forbid, such as a fork, a port to
another platform or a build tuned for particular hardware (a "fork"). Doing so is a permitted purpose for as
long as all of these hold:

a. Open. Every part of the fork that is the software or based on it, your changes included, is offered to
   everyone under Shield with these terms. The complete source for each build you release is public, free of
   charge and without sign-up, from the day of that release. You may leave out a platform maker's SDK that
   you are not allowed to publish.
b. Free. The fork, its builds, updates and features cost nothing, and getting them is not gated by ads,
   sign-ups or payment. Nothing in the fork, or on the page your group offers it from, advertises, sells or
   unlocks anything paid: no ads, sponsor or paid placement, shop, paid cosmetics, pay-to-win, tiers or early
   access. Outside servers, and mods the player added themselves, are not part of the fork, whatever they
   sell. Your group takes no payment for the fork or for anything in it, and does not sell or trade its
   users' data. Donations under 3c, and what section 2 allows, are not payment for the fork.
c. Donations. People may give money to those who make the fork. An ask for it is a plain link or button. It
   never interrupts: no pop-up, notification, timer, or screen the user has to close or wait through. It
   gates nothing, and giving gets the giver nothing in the fork.
d. Whole product. 3b and 3c also apply to everything your group ships the fork in or with, and to any client,
   launcher or installer of your group's that installs, starts or updates it. One counts as your group's when it
   is named or branded after the fork, or is published from the same account, organization or signing key, or
   from a domain your group controls. A server that the fork contains, starts or needs for single-player play is
   part of the fork.
e. Own identity. The fork states that it is based on Sunrise and links to
   https://github.com/sunrise-org/sunrise. If its name contains "Sunrise", it also states that it is
   unofficial, and the word "unofficial" (or its translation) appears beside the name on its download page
   or store listing and on its title or about screen. It does not use the Project's logo or icon as its own, and it does not use the Project's sign-in,
   update, telemetry or signing services unless the Project's published policy allows it.

If you stop meeting these conditions, this permission no longer covers what you do from then on, and Shield's
Violations section applies. Other people's rights are not affected.

## 4. Same terms downstream

Everyone who receives the software, inside a fork or any other way, receives it from its licensors under
Shield with these terms. Nobody can pass it on under other terms, and putting it into a product under another
licence does not change this.

## 5. Later versions

The Project may publish later versions of these terms only by the decision its GOVERNANCE.md requires for a
licence change. A later version may make the wording clearer or add permissions. It may never allow a competing
product that fails 3a, 3b or 3d, never allow dedicated server software to be sold, and never add rights that not
everyone gets. You may use the software under the version it came with or any later one.

